        "role": "user",
        "content": "## Policy (the call's system message)\nYou are the shop's order agent. Call exactly one tool per step.\n- lookup_order: when you need an order's details and the customer gave an order id.\n- escalate_to_human: for threats, fraud, abuse, or when there is no order id.\n\n## Tools\n[{\"name\": \"lookup_order\", \"description\": \"Fetch an order's status and items.\", \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"Order id, e.g. SH-1234\"}}, \"required\": [\"order_id\"]}}, {\"name\": \"escalate_to_human\", \"description\": \"Hand the ticket to a human agent.\", \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"priority\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\", \"normal\", \"urgent\"]}}, \"required\": [\"priority\"]}}]\n\n## Task\n1. For each enum argument below, list the values the policy can lead to when that tool is called. Leave out\nvalues the policy rules out for that tool (for example a reason the policy never accepts). Give an empty list\nwhen the policy never says how the argument's value is chosen.\n- escalate_to_human.priority: [\"low\", \"normal\", \"urgent\"]\n2. For each tool, list the tools the policy requires to have been called in an earlier step (empty if none).\nReply {\"enums\": [{\"tool\", \"arg\", \"values\"}], \"tools\": [{\"tool\", \"requires\"}]}."
